\begin_layout Remark
For a regular surface this is a theorem, and its proof uses the ambient 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{R}^{3}$
\end_inset

. For an abstract set 
\begin_inset Formula $M$
\end_inset

 there is no ambient space, so 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{x}_{\alpha}$
\end_inset

 is differentiable
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 has no meaning. The only maps we can still ask to be differentiable are the changes of parameters, since they go between open subsets of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{R}^{n}$
\end_inset

. This property becomes an axiom in the next definition. 
\end_layout

