\begin_layout Section
Differentiable manifolds in 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

 dimensions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
A 
\emph on
differentiable manifold
\emph default
 of dimension 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

 is a set 
\begin_inset Formula $M$
\end_inset

 together with a family of injective mappings 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{x}_{\alpha}:U_{\alpha}\to M$
\end_inset

 of open sets 
\begin_inset Formula $U_{\alpha}\subset\mathbb{R}^{n}$
\end_inset

 into 
\begin_inset Formula $M$
\end_inset

 such that 
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate

\begin_inset Argument item:1
status collapsed


\begin_layout Standard
(1)
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $\bigcup_{\alpha}\mathbf{x}_{\alpha}(U_{\alpha})=M$
\end_inset

; 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\begin_inset Argument item:1
status collapsed


\begin_layout Standard
(2)
\end_layout

\end_inset

for all 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha,\beta$
\end_inset

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $W=\mathbf{x}_{\alpha}(U_{\alpha})\cap\mathbf{x}_{\beta}(U_{\beta})\neq\emptyset$
\end_inset

, the sets 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{x}_{\alpha}^{-1}(W)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{x}_{\beta}^{-1}(W)$
\end_inset

 are open subsets of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{R}^{n}$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{x}_{\beta}^{-1}\circ\mathbf{x}_{\alpha}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{x}_{\alpha}^{-1}\circ\mathbf{x}_{\beta}$
\end_inset

 are differentiable functions; 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\begin_inset Argument item:1
status collapsed


\begin_layout Standard
(3)
\end_layout

\end_inset

the family 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ (U_{\alpha},\mathbf{x}_{\alpha})\right\} $
\end_inset

 is maximal relative to conditions (1) and (2). 
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Definition
The pair 
\begin_inset Formula $(U_{\alpha},\mathbf{x}_{\alpha})$
\end_inset

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $p\in\mathbf{x}_{\alpha}(U_{\alpha})$
\end_inset

 is called a 
\emph on
parametrization
\emph default
 (or system of coordinates) of 
\begin_inset Formula $M$
\end_inset

 at 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

. A family 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ (U_{\alpha},\mathbf{x}_{\alpha})\right\} $
\end_inset

 satisfying (1) and (2) is called a 
\emph on
differentiable structure
\emph default
 on 
\begin_inset Formula $M$
\end_inset

. 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Here 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

differentiable
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 means 
\begin_inset Formula $C^{\infty}$
\end_inset

. Condition (1) says the parametrizations cover 
\begin_inset Formula $M$
\end_inset

, and condition (2) is the compatibility of Remark
\begin_inset space ~

\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "rem:change"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

, now imposed as an axiom.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Remark

\begin_inset Argument 1
status collapsed


\begin_layout Standard
On maximality
\end_layout

\end_inset

Condition (3) is about the family of parametrizations, not about the size of the sets 
\begin_inset Formula $U_{\alpha}$
\end_inset

: any parametrization 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{y}:U\to M$
\end_inset

 that satisfies (2) together with every member of the family is already in the family. It is purely technical: a family satisfying (1) and (2) can always be completed to a maximal one by adding all parametrizations compatible with it. So in practice we define a manifold by giving a differentiable structure, as in the examples below. 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset Separator plain

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Remark

\begin_inset Argument 1
status collapsed


\begin_layout Standard
Topology on 
\begin_inset Formula $M$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $M$
\end_inset

 is only a set, no topology is given. A differentiable structure induces one: declare 
\begin_inset Formula $A\subset M$
\end_inset

 to be 
\emph on
open
\emph default
 if and only if 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\mathbf{x}_{\alpha}^{-1}\left(A\cap\mathbf{x}_{\alpha}(U_{\alpha})\right)\text{ is open in }\mathbb{R}^{n}\text{ for all }\alpha
\]
\end_inset

With this topology the sets 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{x}_{\alpha}(U_{\alpha})$
\end_inset

 are open and the maps 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{x}_{\alpha}$
\end_inset

 are continuous. 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Example: the real projective space
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
The real projective space of dimension 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\mathbb{R}P^{n}:=\left\{ \text{lines through the origin in }\mathbb{R}^{n+1}\right\} =\left(\mathbb{R}^{n+1}\setminus\left\{ 0\right\} \right)/\sim
\]
\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $u\sim\lambda u$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda\in\mathbb{R}^{*}=\mathbb{R}\setminus\left\{ 0\right\} $
\end_inset

. We write 
\begin_inset Formula $[x_{0},\dots,x_{n}]$
\end_inset

 for the class of 
\begin_inset Formula $(x_{0},\dots,x_{n})$
\end_inset

. 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset Float figure
placement h
wide false
sideways false
status open


\begin_layout Standard
\align center
\begin_inset Graphics 
	filename geodiff_2026-7_lecture01_figures/projective.pdf
	width 75text%

\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Caption Standard

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Left: every line through the origin of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{R}^{2}$
\end_inset

 meets the upper half circle once, except the horizontal line, which meets both endpoints. Gluing the endpoints gives 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{R}P^{1}\cong S^{1}$
\end_inset

. Right: every line through the origin of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{R}^{3}$
\end_inset

 meets 
\begin_inset Formula $S^{2}$
\end_inset

 in two antipodal points 
\begin_inset Formula $\pm u$
\end_inset

, so 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{R}P^{2}=S^{2}/(u\sim-u)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "fig:projective"

\end_inset

 
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Parametrizations.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{0}\neq0$
\end_inset

 we can choose 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda=1/x_{0}$
\end_inset

: 
\begin_inset Formula \[
[x_{0},x_{1},\dots,x_{n}]=\left[1,\frac{x_{1}}{x_{0}},\dots,\frac{x_{n}}{x_{0}}\right]
\]
\end_inset

so such a point is determined by 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

 real numbers. Define 
\begin_inset Formula \[
V_{0}:=\left\{ [x_{0},\dots,x_{n}]\mid x_{0}\neq0\right\}
\]
\end_inset

and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{x}_{0}:\mathbb{R}^{n}\to V_{0}$
\end_inset

 by 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\mathbf{x}_{0}(x_{1},\dots,x_{n})=[1,x_{1},\dots,x_{n}]
\]
\end_inset

which is injective (in fact a bijection onto 
\begin_inset Formula $V_{0}$
\end_inset

). In the same way, for 
\begin_inset Formula $i=0,\dots,n$
\end_inset

, let 
\begin_inset Formula \[
V_{i}:=\left\{ [x_{0},\dots,x_{n}]\mid x_{i}\neq0\right\}
\]
\end_inset

and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{x}_{i}:\mathbb{R}^{n}\to V_{i}$
\end_inset

 (insert a 
\begin_inset Formula $1$
\end_inset

 in slot 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

) 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\mathbf{x}_{i}(y_{0},\dots,y_{i-1},y_{i+1},\dots,y_{n})=[y_{0},\dots,y_{i-1},1,y_{i+1},\dots,y_{n}]
\]
\end_inset

with inverse 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\mathbf{x}_{i}^{-1}:[x_{0},\dots,x_{n}]\mapsto\left(\frac{x_{0}}{x_{i}},\dots,\frac{x_{i-1}}{x_{i}},\frac{x_{i+1}}{x_{i}},\dots,\frac{x_{n}}{x_{i}}\right)
\]
\end_inset

which does not depend on the representative (multiplying all 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{k}$
\end_inset

 by 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset

 does not change the ratios).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Condition (1).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Every 
\begin_inset Formula $u\neq0$
\end_inset

 has some nonzero coordinate, so 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\bigcup_{i=0}^{n}V_{i}=\mathbb{R}P^{n}
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Condition (2).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $i\neq j$
\end_inset

. A point 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{x}_{j}(y)$
\end_inset

 lies in 
\begin_inset Formula $V_{i}$
\end_inset

 iff its 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

-th entry is nonzero, so 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\mathbf{x}_{j}^{-1}(V_{i}\cap V_{j})=\left\{ (y_{0},\dots,y_{j-1},y_{j+1},\dots,y_{n})\in\mathbb{R}^{n}\mid y_{i}\neq0\right\}
\]
\end_inset

is open in 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{R}^{n}$
\end_inset

 (the entry 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{j}$
\end_inset

 is omitted). On this set 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\mathbf{x}_{i}^{-1}\circ\mathbf{x}_{j}(y_{0},\dots,y_{j-1},y_{j+1},\dots,y_{n})=\left(\frac{y_{0}}{y_{i}},\dots,\frac{y_{j-1}}{y_{i}},\frac{1}{y_{i}},\frac{y_{j+1}}{y_{i}},\dots,\frac{y_{n}}{y_{i}}\right)
\]
\end_inset

where the entry 
\begin_inset Formula $1/y_{i}$
\end_inset

 sits in slot 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

 and the entry 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}/y_{i}=1$
\end_inset

 (slot 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

) is omitted. All components are rational functions with nonvanishing denominator, hence differentiable.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
So 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ (\mathbb{R}^{n},\mathbf{x}_{i})\right\} _{i=0}^{n}$
\end_inset

 is a differentiable structure, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{R}P^{n}$
\end_inset

 is a differentiable manifold of dimension 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Differentiable maps between manifolds
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Our next goal is to say when two manifolds are 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

the same
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 (isomorphic). For this we first need differentiable maps between manifolds. Differentiability of a map on 
\begin_inset Formula $M$
\end_inset

 can only be tested through parametrizations, i.e. by going down to 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{R}^{n}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $M_{1}^{n}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $M_{2}^{m}$
\end_inset

 be differentiable manifolds of dimensions 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $m$
\end_inset

. A mapping 
\begin_inset Formula $\varphi:M_{1}\to M_{2}$
\end_inset

 is 
\emph on
differentiable at
\emph default
 
\begin_inset Formula $p\in M_{1}$
\end_inset

 if given a parametrization 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{y}:V\subset\mathbb{R}^{m}\to M_{2}$
\end_inset

 at 
\begin_inset Formula $\varphi(p)$
\end_inset

 there exists a parametrization 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{x}:U\subset\mathbb{R}^{n}\to M_{1}$
\end_inset

 at 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $\varphi(\mathbf{x}(U))\subset\mathbf{y}(V)$
\end_inset

 and the mapping 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\mathbf{y}^{-1}\circ\varphi\circ\mathbf{x}:U\subset\mathbb{R}^{n}\to\mathbb{R}^{m}
\]
\end_inset

is differentiable at 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{x}^{-1}(p)$
\end_inset

. A mapping which is differentiable at all points of 
\begin_inset Formula $M_{1}$
\end_inset

 is called 
\emph on
differentiable
\emph default
. 
\end_layout

