\begin_layout Standard
The condition 
\begin_inset Formula $\varphi(\mathbf{x}(U))\subset\mathbf{y}(V)$
\end_inset

 makes the composition 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{y}^{-1}\circ\varphi\circ\mathbf{x}$
\end_inset

 defined on all of 
\begin_inset Formula $U$
\end_inset

. This is why 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{y}$
\end_inset

 is chosen first and then 
\begin_inset Formula $U$
\end_inset

 is taken small enough.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Question.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Does differentiability of 
\begin_inset Formula $\varphi:M_{1}\to M_{2}$
\end_inset

 depend on the choices made? 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
It does 
\emph on
not
\emph default
 depend on the choice of parametrizations. If 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{x}',\mathbf{y}'$
\end_inset

 are other parametrizations at 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\varphi(p)$
\end_inset

, then near 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{x}'^{-1}(p)$
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\mathbf{y}'^{-1}\circ\varphi\circ\mathbf{x}'=\left(\mathbf{y}'^{-1}\circ\mathbf{y}\right)\circ\left(\mathbf{y}^{-1}\circ\varphi\circ\mathbf{x}\right)\circ\left(\mathbf{x}^{-1}\circ\mathbf{x}'\right)
\]
\end_inset

and the outer two factors are changes of parameters, differentiable by condition (2). This is exactly why (2) is part of the definition. 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
It 
\emph on
does
\emph default
 depend on the differentiable structures. For example, on 
\begin_inset Formula $M=\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

 take the structure generated by 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{x}(t)=t$
\end_inset

 and the one generated by 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{y}(t)=t^{3}$
\end_inset

. Then 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{y}^{-1}\circ\mathrm{id}\circ\mathbf{x}(t)=t^{1/3}$
\end_inset

 is not differentiable at 
\begin_inset Formula $0$
\end_inset

, so 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{id}:(\mathbb{R},\mathbf{x})\to(\mathbb{R},\mathbf{y})$
\end_inset

 is not differentiable, and the two structures are different. (Still, 
\begin_inset Formula $\varphi(t)=t^{3}$
\end_inset

 from 
\begin_inset Formula $(\mathbb{R},\mathbf{x})$
\end_inset

 to 
\begin_inset Formula $(\mathbb{R},\mathbf{y})$
\end_inset

 is differentiable with differentiable inverse, since in coordinates both read 
\begin_inset Formula $t\mapsto t$
\end_inset

. So the two manifolds are 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

the same
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

, which leads to the notion of isomorphism we are after.) 
\end_layout

